<task id="014" project="spaarke-intake-pipeline-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-07/FR-09 plumbing: stage engine inputs via BlobGateway, send ids-only Job Contract to the engine queue, consume the completion message, enforce the hard timeout (run Failed with reason)</title>
    <phase>1 Pipeline core and first doors</phase>
    <gate>blocked — tasks 013, 001, 002</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>New BFF .cs (dispatcher, completion and timeout handlers) on the claim-check path; depends on 3 tasks; tags bff-api, worker.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Wiring between defined contracts (013 stage slots, 001 envelope, 002 queue/container) with explicit references (BlobGateway, JobSubmissionService, IJobHandler).</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>P1-C</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>file-overlap with task 016 (Infrastructure/DI/JobProcessingModule.cs); run 014 and 016 one after the other or rebase the second.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>013, 001, 002</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, worker, job, azure, unit-test, integration-test</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-8 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Connect the orchestrator to the engine job: stage the source `.docx` and the edit plan in the stamp-private staging container through `Infrastructure/Storage/BlobGateway.cs`, send an ids-only Job Contract to `intake-engine-requests`, consume the engine's completion Job Contract from its own queue `intake-engine-completions` with a dedicated processor that accepts only `IntakeEngineCompleted`, treat each completion as untrusted input, advance the run, and enforce a 10-minute hard timeout with a scheduled timeout message that fails the run with a reason if no completion arrived.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in Service Bus (scheduled enqueue), Azure Blob with managed identity, ADR-004 jobs and claim-check staging. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>
  <goal>`Services/Intake/Engine/IntakeEngineDispatcher.cs` (stage + dispatch, idempotent on run id), `IntakeEngineCompletionJobHandler.cs` (JobType `IntakeEngineCompleted`), `IntakeEngineCompletionProcessor.cs` (hosted Service Bus consumer of `intake-engine-completions`, copied from `CommunicationJobProcessor`) and `IntakeEngineTimeoutJobHandler.cs` (JobType `IntakeEngineTimeout`), an engine-queue send and scheduled-enqueue method on `JobSubmissionService`, registered in `JobProcessingModule.cs`; deterministic staging paths `intake-staging/{runId}/in/…` and output paths `…/out/…` agreed with task 001; tests for dispatch, completion, duplicate completion, late completion after timeout, and timeout.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Spec FR-07, FR-09 (engine step), NFR-03 (claim check, no content in messages/logs, 7-day TTL), NFR-05 (hard timeout → held/failed with admin attention), NFR-06 (correlationId BFF → queue → job → completion); ADR-004 A2 §3. Task 002 creates `intake-engine-requests` and `intake-engine-completions` and runs the job as its OWN identity (never the stamp/BFF identity), with Sender on the completion queue and no right on any BFF queue (owner 2026-11-25: Service Bus Data Sender is per queue, not per message type, and a message does not carry its sender's identity, so send rights on `sdap-jobs` would let a compromised engine enqueue any BFF job type). The BFF consumes the completion queue with a dedicated processor, the per-domain pattern `CommunicationJobProcessor` already uses for `sdap-communication`: a closed `JobType` switch, anything else dead-lettered `WrongQueue`. The timeout message is the BFF's own and goes to the BFF jobs queue as before. The envelope fields and output-path convention are defined by task 001's `entrypoint/envelope.py`. The NDA harness (015) calls the dispatcher from its Build stage; 013 owns the run state transitions this task's handlers trigger.</background>
